VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of full screen, available at buzz.xyz. Now, Buzz effectively is just Slack, okay? It\'s like an agentic Slack where you can have different AI agents join, but the architecture is what makes it really interesting in terms of how the agents communicate and how they actually'

[01:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'connect and join. So, what I want to do is explain that architecture to you, and then I\'m going to get into how you host it. Now, the main thing to understand in Buzz is that it\'s a lot different than having like a Slack bot or something. The agents that are in here, they have'

[01:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'their own identity, and they\'re tied to a human owner. So, you come in as a human, and you can chat in the channel, right, and ask the agents to do stuff, but those agents are associated with you. Now, you can have agents join from'

[01:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'somewhere else as well, but the idea is kind of like bring your own agent. So, I\'m Tim, I join into this kind of Buzz community, you know, just like a Slack workspace or whatever, and from there, I can actually connect up any of the agents that are running on my computer.'

[01:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, I can bring my Claude code agent. I I bring my Codex agent. I can bring a local model or local agent on my computer from LM Studio or something and tie that directly into the Buzz community. So, if you have multiple'

[02:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'teammates, they can all bring their own agents. And that means that all of those agents can have their own configuration like their own skills, their own MCP servers, whatever the owner of that agent has set up. Now, remote agents can join as well, but typically what you\'re'

[02:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going to do is have a human join and then the human can bring one or all of their different agents inside of the community and the agents are not tied directly to where it\'s hosted. They can come in from anywhere. You\'ll see what I mean in a second. Now, also every agent'

[02:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and person has scoped permissions per channel and per repo. Buzz actually has a native Git and Git work tree integration, so we can actually host Git repos for you automatically and that allows, you know, agents to work on'

[02:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'coding projects pretty seamlessly. Also, every action is cryptographically signed and logged. I\'ll get into how that works in a minute, but it\'s similar to a blockchain where anything that\'s been done can be proved, is kind of'

[02:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reproducible, and you have a history and kind of an audit log, so you know who\'s doing what and what agents are communicating. And then one agent can actually trigger other agents. This is one of the coolest parts, that they can chat with each other without needing your, kind of, I don\'t know, command'

[03:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'inside approval, whatever. Okay, so let\'s go through the architecture here because this is where most people uh just completely mess this up, especially in the videos that they\'ve been making. Now, what you need to understand is there\'s multiple pieces of software here when it comes to Buzz. Again, think of'

[03:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Buzz like Slack, okay? So, we have the Slack desktop application or the Buzz desktop app. This is where you\'re actually going to communicate, right? You\'re going to see the agents, you\'re going to chat with stuff. So, you'

[03:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'yourself, the human, will download the Buzz desktop application. This is the first step. Once you have it downloaded, you can hook up your different agents, so Claude, Code, Codex, whatever\'s downloaded on your computer. But then, you need to actually join a community.'

[03:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It\'s called Buzz community, but think of it like, you know, a Slack workspace. Now, that workspace needs to be hosted somewhere. Now, you could run this Buzz workspace on your own computer. The issue with that is if you turn off the computer, you lose internet, or'

[03:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'whatever, then it\'s going to go down. So, it\'s a lot better to run this on something like a virtual private server, where effectively it\'s in the cloud and you can connect to it from anywhere. You can join from your phone. You don\'t have to have one device running. You get the idea. So, effectively, we have this main'

[04:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'piece of software, right? Which is kind of like this Buzz, I don\'t know, orchestrator. In fact, they\'re calling it like the Buzz relay. This needs to be running somewhere, because this actually hosts your community. Then, you have the desktop application, and the desktop'

[04:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'application allows you to join this community. So, you can kind of see the diagram here, right? We have a virtual private server. On the server, we have the Buzz app and all of the software there. We have what\'s called the Buzz relay. This essentially brokers the'

[04:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'messages between agents, humans, and whatever\'s joining the workspace. And then on there, you have other things like a Postgres database, Redis, storage media, all of the Git, the workflow, searching through stuff, the database. All of that is hosted, typically, in the'

[04:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cloud in this kind of workspace software. So, Buzz itself actually isn\'t AI at all. It\'s really just allowing AI agents and humans to communicate with each other and live in a workspace. It\'s effectively like an open-source Slack'

[05:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that\'s designed for agentic workflows. Now, the one thing also is that every action that\'s done, like I mentioned, is logged and kept track of, because this uses something called a private public key pair in the background, which allows you to actually have secure'

[05:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'authentication without passwords, without credentials, and just using this kind of string of tokens, which we\'ll look at in a minute. Okay, so last piece of clarity here. The agents that join, typically, are just going to run on the same computer as you, and they\'re tied'

[05:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
